 you get the server rendered version  and then from there on you get the  client rendered version so the  navigation feels instantaneous and  native and so I started thinking what  could we take the same spelt philosophy  of compilers and static analysis and  apply it to that problem and yes we can  Ramez my own so I'm going to create a  new separate project sakura's short for  spelt an app maker actually you know  what I'm gonna go to the here's one I  made earlier so then we don't need to  wait for dependencies to install and if  I build my app then this is this is the  basic starter template and you can see  as before we've got the client side  rendering which is nice and slick it's  very fast but if we look at the the  actual market that you get from the  server it's it's server render which  means that you get that very fast  initial initial load if I were to build  this  then we can have a look at the the  production stats for this  and you can see that it's less than  seven kilobytes  remember you can't see it's quite small  but it's less than seven kilobytes of  JavaScript which is still like more than  is necessary but it's a pretty good  start for reference if we look at the  the same app bill using next then it's  well over 200 kilobytes which is frankly  too much for an app that doesn't yet do  a great deal and I have some numbers on  the server-side rendering performance so  I don't want to get too hung up on the  performance stuff I want to I want to  promote the idea that we should be  comparing frameworks along other more  interesting dimensions such as those  this framework helped me build a more  accessible web I haven't shown you this  but spelt and will warn you if you build  if you use things that aren't accessible  does it help us build slick user  interfaces does it help us write fewer  lines of code and can we write tools  that are more sophisticated than stuff  that's based on the command line I've  been very inspired by these two projects  so in my remaining 60 seconds I am going  to try and show you what I've been  working on and this is a a graphical  user interface for building sapper amps  and you can do some pretty cool stuff if  you have tools that are deeply  integrated with the frameworks that they  serve  I can open an editor and I can go  straight to the page that I'm currently  on if I want to look at a particular  element and edit that then I can click  the the crosshairs button and it'll take  me straight to the source code so I can  go ahead and edit it which is a huge  productivity room I've been building  apps like this for short while and it is  transformed and how quickly I'm able  able to build stuff and so in summary if  the first era was the agrarian age in  which homesteaders tended to their code  bases by hand and the second era was the  Industrial Age when we started to use  machines for everything and people  worried a little bit about the pollution  but we can't deny that it had this  transformative effect on productivity  the  the third age is the information age the  machines are becoming intelligent and  this is the point at which we can stop  and say how can computers help us  fulfill our potential as creative  professionals I think this is some very  largely unexplored territory it doesn't  just apply to user interface it also  applies to tools and everything else  that we work on and so I hope that you  will come and explore it with me and  with that thank you
 

